  "html": "<div id=\"questionExplanation-123620\" class=\"questionExplanation\" style=\"display: block;\"><p>First, we recall that:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>An angle in the standard position has its initial side on the positive <mjx-container class=\"MathJax CtxtMenu_Attached_0\" jax=\"CHTML\" tabindex=\"0\" ctxtmenu_counter=\"22\" style=\"font-size: 113.1%; position: relative;\"><mjx-math class=\"MJX-TEX\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><mjx-mi class=\"mjx-i\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c1D465 TEX-I\"></mjx-c></mjx-mi></mjx-math><mjx-assistive-mml unselectable=\"on\" display=\"inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi></math></mjx-assistive-mml></mjx-container>-axis, and its vertex is at the origin. </p>\n</li>\n<li><p>It rotates such that a positive angle is measured counter-clockwise.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Therefore, the correct diagram is as follows:</p>\n<div id=\"graphic-q-123620-a-1\" class=\"graphic\"><img src=\"/graphics/q-123620-a-1\"></div></div>",
